WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.720
It would be a tall order to weave the galaxy’s
most feared Sith Lord back into Star Wars

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.130
Episode 9, but the dark side of the Force
is a pathway to many abilities some consider

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.500
to be… unnatural.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.680
So, how exactly might that happen?

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.880
Here are a few ideas.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.120
George Lucas inserted Anakin Skywalker as
a Force ghost in the final moments of Return

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:19.840
of the Jedi.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:25.000
Although it's always been assumed that only
Jedi could access this power, Anakin's last-minute

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.390
switch to the "good guys" must have given
him just enough positive mojo to earn a spot

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.940
beside Obi-Wan and Yoda.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.640
Replacing Sebastian Shaw with a digitally
inserted Hayden Christensen in the special

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.640
edition still feels like a gratuitous revision
to the classic film, but it does crack the

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.239
door open for what might be the most likely
way for Vader to return in Episode 9, as a

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.180
Force ghost played by Christensen.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.300
Whether offering counsel to Rey as a Jedi,
or to Kylo Ren as the new Supreme Leader of

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.480
the First Order, Anakin could still impact
his family's story one last time.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.940
In The Art of The Force Awakens, concept art
from Episode 7 by Ian McCaig portrays Christensen

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.360
as Anakin appearing as a Force ghost, with
his face alternating between a human visage

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.500
and the mask of Vader.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.800
This piece was created after Abrams came on
board as director, so it's entirely possible

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.280
he could revisit the concept in Episode IX.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.880
Watch the Star Wars saga films enough, and
soon enough you’ll start to notice that

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.710
certain aspects of the movies "rhyme," with
certain plot beats echoing across all three

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.300
trilogies.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.320
“Again it’s like poetry so that they rhyme.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.260
Every stanza kinda rhymes with the last one.”

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.850
For example, there's a climactic moment in
each of the first films where a trusted mentor

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.340
figure dies unexpectedly.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.880
Revenge of the Sith and Return of the Jedi
both feature unexpected character reveals

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:42.640
of some kind.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:43.940
Will the pattern repeat?

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.700
Okay, so that’s probably a massive stretch,
but what good is a fan theory without some

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.120
grasping at straws?

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.300
“I’m getting too old for this sort of
thing.”

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.020
Following the reveal theme, Episode 9 could
feature a sequence in which a ghostly Vader

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:01.659
is providing counsel to Kylo Ren, secretly
guiding him toward a decisive loss at the

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.119
hands of the Resistance… only for Ren to
learn the ghost is actually the Force ghost

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.840
of Luke Skywalker, tricking Kylo to help his
friends?

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.859
If nothing else, it would be a suitable follow-up
to Luke’s promise that he’ll see Kylo

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.520
again at the end of The Last Jedi.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.920
In The Force Awakens, Rey experiences what
would later be termed a "Forceback," in which

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.209
her contact with Luke's lightsaber gives her
a glimpse of both the past and future of the

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:25.209
Star Wars saga.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:32.560
“That lightsaber was Luke's and his father's
before him, and now it calls to you.”

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.359
Filled with references to Star Wars past and
present, this sequence has provided fertile

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.620
ground for Star Wars fan speculators.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.680
A flashback in Episode 9 would offer another
easy way for Abrams to introduce Darth Vader

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.600
back into the saga.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:50.800
Kylo could get a glimpse of a pivotal moment
in Vader's life through contact with his helmet,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.360
or another character could remember their
own encounter with Vader from the past.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.200
One piece of old universe lore that has been
reintroduced is the holocron: a small device,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.150
usually a cube, that can hold training and
insights from either Jedi or Sith.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.180
When the holocron is activated, the recording
is played back as a hologram, similar to Princess

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.300
Leia projecting from R2-D2 in the original
Star Wars.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:18.060
The holocron was introduced in 1991 in the
Dark Empire comics series and returned to

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.680
modern continuity in later comics and the
Clone Wars and Rebels television series.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.480
It's a handy plot device, whether as a "MacGuffin"
to prompt a Jedi or Sith race across the galaxy,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.090
or an easy way to cram in some exposition
through the broadcast of a long-lost character.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.960
More interestingly, if Vader recorded his
own holocron of Sith insights, it's possible

00:03:37.969 --> 00:03:41.510
Kylo Ren might have discovered it, and could
replay it in Episode 9.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:47.159
In Revenge of the Sith, Palpatine explains
to Anakin that Darth Plagueis was a Sith Lord

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:51.879
obsessed with immortality who mastered the
ability to create life through the manipulation

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.000
of midichlorians.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.140
Plagueis was once Darth Sidious' Sith master,
and as soon as Sidious mastered his own grasp

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.840
of this arcane art, he murdered Plagueis in
his sleep.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.049
A single panel in the "Fortress Vader" storyline
from the Darth Vader comics series connects

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:11.470
this Sith legend to one of the biggest questions
in Star Wars lore, the parentage of Anakin

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:11.960
Skywalker.

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.919
We know Anakin's mother was Shmi, a slave
from Tattooine, but his father has always

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.080
remained a mystery.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:23.270
In Vader's journey through Mustafar's mysterious
dark side portal, he has a vision of his mother

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.180
as Palpatine lurks just over her shoulder,
manipulating strange energies in her womb.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.460
It's the closest Star Wars has come to implying
that the "father" of Anakin Skywalker is in

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.180
fact Emperor Palpatine, using the powers he
learned from Darth Plagueis to create life.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.420
It's possible this knowledge didn't die with
Palpatine, and that he passed it on somehow,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.480
either to his apprentice or within a holocron
or other vessel.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.420
Kylo’s discovery of such knowledge would
set him on the path to mastering it and resurrecting

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.420
his grandfather.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.300
It's very clear that Episode 9's filmmakers
could bring back Vader if they wanted to.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.180
But should they?

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.520
It's a tough question.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.800
On the one hand, this new Star Wars trilogy
has managed to cleverly fold in elements of

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.440
the past films in surprising and resonant
ways.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.850
If they do bring back Darth Vader, it's sure
to feel significant and creatively fulfilling.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.699
And if you view the total nine-film story
of the mainline Star Wars films as the saga

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:20.150
of the Skywalker family, then it would make
sense for the patriarch to return one last

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:23.580
time and influence the events surrounding
his grandson's rise to power.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:29.060
On the other hand, Vader has become iconic
partly because when he does appear, it truly

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:29.560
matters.

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:34.599
Take that sequence in Rogue One, sure, it
was a nice excuse for an extended sequence

00:05:34.599 --> 00:05:38.889
of Sith badassery using all of the special
effects and filmmaking skills the crew had

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:39.380
to offer.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:43.860
But it was also a demonstration of Vader’s
impact on a moment of key importance to the

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.660
entire Star Wars galaxy.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.680
If he had succeeded, the Death Star plans
would have remained in the Empire's possession,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.040
and the burgeoning Rebellion might have been
snuffed out.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.680
His failure had immense consequences for the
story as a whole.

00:05:57.249 --> 00:06:00.880
Ultimately, that's the real question fans
should be asking about Darth Vader in the

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.180
run-up to Episode 9.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.039
Not whether he can return to the story, but
instead what kind of monumental event could

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.100
warrant his reappearance in a galaxy far,
far away.

